Architcture of CORBA Java?
=========================

The architecture of CORBA in Java explains how distributed objects communicate across networks using the Object Request Broker (ORB). Here’s a clear breakdown of the main components and how they interact.

CORBA Java Architecture
========================
1. Core Idea: ORB (Object Request Broker)
=========================================
    The ORB is the middleware that handles communication between client and server objects.
    It lets a client call methods on remote objects as if they were local.

2. Main Components
==================
    Client Side
    ===========
    Client Application
    Calls methods on remote CORBA objects.
    Stub (Client Proxy)
    Generated from IDL.
    Converts method calls into a format the ORB can send.
    ORB (Client Side)
    Sends the request to the server.

    Server Side
    ===========
    Skeleton (Server-side proxy)
    Also generated from IDL (e.g., HelloPOA).
    Receives requests and calls actual implementation.
    Servant (Implementation Class)
    Your class (e.g., HelloImpl.java) that implements methods.
    ORB (Server Side)
    Receives requests and routes them to the servant.
 
    Shared Components
    =================
    IDL (Interface Definition Language)
    Language-neutral interface definition (e.g., Hello.idl).
    IDL Compiler (idlj)
    Generates Java bindings (stubs & skeletons).
    Object Adapter (POA – Portable Object Adapter)
    Connects ORB with servant objects.
    Handles object lifecycle, activation, and request dispatching.
    Naming Service
    Allows clients to find server objects by name.

3. Communication Flow
======================
    Client calls method on stub
    Stub sends request to client ORB
    ORB transmits request over network
    Server ORB receives request
    Request goes to POA
    POA forwards to skeleton
    Skeleton invokes method on servant (HelloImpl)
    Result is sent back the same path

4. Simple Diagram (Conceptual)
==============================
Client Side                         Server Side
-------------                      ----------------
Client App                         Servant (HelloImpl)
     |                                   ^
     v                                   |
   Stub  ------------------------>   Skeleton (HelloPOA)
     |                                   ^
     v                                   |
   ORB  <------------------------>      ORB
                  Network
                  (IIOP)
           POA + Naming Service

5. Key Technologies
===================
    IIOP (Internet Inter-ORB Protocol) → Communication protocol used by CORBA
    POA (Portable Object Adapter) → Manages server objects
    ORB → Core communication engine



6. Key Advantages
=================
    Language-independent (IDL-based)
    Platform-independent
    Supports distributed systems

